title Monocypher-parity for constant-time verify, GREEN
prompt read REMEMBER + the 1024-round itinerary, the six-season double-seat, and the rye-first-crypto decision wave; take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, Rye-first, send each increment as its own signed round; stop at custody gates
think Walked the decision wave and the last five session logs. Season G (Cryptography · Rye-native, Monocypher-parity) is the active double-seat and the highest-Lindy new thread — it serves every module that signs, verifies, or seals. Fourteen Monocypher-source parity rungs stood GREEN; the clean one-shot primitives were nearly exhausted. The prior log named the crux-first next rung outright: Monocypher's constant-time crypto_verify16/32/64 equality primitives.
think Confirmed the gap is real, not busywork: crypto/ had no constant-time compare, and crypto/hmac_sha512.rye and crypto/hmac_sha256.rye each explicitly named "verifying a MAC wants a constant-time compare, which the composer that uses this primitive supplies" as an open horizon. This primitive is that supplier — the highest-Lindy piece left, since every MAC, tag, and signature-equality check reaches for it, and it is the one corner of the Season's constant-time horizon correct by STRUCTURE rather than by metal timing measurement.
think Weighed the alternatives and rejected them for scope: raw standalone XChaCha20 (crypto_chacha20_x) needs the DJB 64-bit-counter variant our IETF-only chacha20.rye lacks — new crypto, not a clean harness; Elligator2 is a large new primitive with its own constant-time subtlety. crypto_verify is small, foundational, thematically central, and a clean Monocypher parity target.
think Rye-first: authored crypto/verify.rye from the idea, not a copied line. Two byte strings are equal iff the OR of their byte-wise XORs is zero; the fold diff |= a[i] ^ b[i] visits every byte with no early exit, so the loop count depends on the public length and never on the possibly-secret contents, and the final zero-test (x | -x) >> 7 reads any difference — high bit or low — as one branch-free flag. Exposed the generic ct_equal (with LengthMismatch / TooLong edges) and the three fixed-width doors verify16/32/64 matching Monocypher's signatures, all over one shared ct_equal_core.
think Proved it three ways. In-module selftest against Zig's independent std.crypto.timing_safe.eql: at all three Monocypher widths (16, 32, 64) an equal span compares equal and flipping EVERY single bit of EVERY byte compares unequal, our answer matching Zig's in every one of those cases; the fixed-width doors agree with the generic engine; the edge refuses a length mismatch and an over-long compare before a byte is folded. Then the fifteenth Monocypher-source parity rung as the established three-file pattern: a C harness linking the unmodified vendored crypto_verify16/32/64, a Rye printing harness over verify.rye through a new tools/verify.rye symlink, and a witness diffing fifteen equal/not-equal verdicts byte-for-byte, anchored to Monocypher's own 0-for-equal / -1-for-differing contract.
obs GREEN — crypto/verify.rye selftest GREEN against Zig; the parity witness diffs Monocypher and Rye fifteen verdicts identical and the sampled 0/-1 contract holds. The primitive holds no key and signs nothing; purely local, no network, no funds, no device. Constant-time timing-safety stays the Season's named horizon — correctness proven here, not a timing claim boasted.
obs This lap ADDED a crypto file (unlike the recent embedded-mode rungs), so the count guard moved 56 → 57 (thirty-six primitives + twenty-one compositions). Registered crypto_verify_witness in tools/crypto_suite_witness.rish; count guard GREEN at 57; the full crypto suite re-greened whole. TAME style check GREEN — the authored Rye carries the opening triad and ≥2 invariant-commented asserts per fn, bounded buffers, a named-error edge.
obs Rishi string-escaping teaching: printf '%s\n' and shell loop vars ${n}/$n collide with Rishi's own ${...} interpolation and dash's \n handling; the robust idiom is line-literal sed -n 'Np' into Rishi lets, exactly as the poly1305 witness anchors. Folded that into the witness's contract anchor.
loom witness=crypto_monocypher_verify_parity lines=15 widths=3 diff=identical
